spm - the SwirlOS package manager
=================================

spm installs small packages (terminal commands, data files, anything that is
just files) from a git repository, over HTTPS, with every file verified
against a SHA-256 hash from the repository's index.

  spm update               refresh the package index
  spm search [term]        list or search packages
  spm info <pkg>           details, files and dependencies
  spm install <pkg...>     install packages (+ dependencies)
  spm remove <pkg...>      remove packages (refuses if something needs them)
  spm upgrade [pkg...]     upgrade installed packages to the index version
  spm list                 installed packages
  spm repo [set <src>]     show or change the package source
  spm os [check|upgrade|verify|repair|status]   SwirlOS system updates (see below)
  spm repo reset           back to the default source

  -f                       overwrite files not owned by the package / ignore deps

Package sources (spm repo set ...):
  owner/repo[@branch]      GitHub repository (raw.githubusercontent.com)
  https://host/path/       any web folder that holds index.json
  /some/folder             a local folder or USB stick that holds index.json

Default source: qwerwasi6-lab/swirl-packages@main

Safety
  * HTTPS is verified against the built-in CA bundle (add more roots in
    /system/spm/ca.pem if you use a private CA).
  * Files are downloaded to "<dest>.spm-new", hash-checked, and only then
    moved into place. A failed download never leaves a half-written file.
  * spm never overwrites a file it does not own (use -f to force) and never
    writes under /system/kernel or /system/spm.

State lives in /system/spm (index.json, repo, db/<pkg>.json).
Registry layout and how to publish: see the README in the repository.
Terminal commands installed by spm go to /system/gterm and work immediately.

System updates (spm os)
  spm os check             is a newer SwirlOS release available? (Settings > Software Update does this too)
  spm os upgrade           download ONLY the system files that changed, verify them, install
  spm os verify            compare every system file with the install record (offline)
  spm os repair            restore missing or changed system files from the release
  spm os status            installed version
  -f                       allow going back to an older release

  * The install record is /system/spm/os.tsv (version + SHA-256 of every system file).
  * Every file is SHA-256 verified before anything is touched. If a download fails or
    does not match, nothing changes.
  * Install is journaled: old files are kept as <file>.spm-old until the whole update
    is in. A failure or power cut rolls back to the old, working system - at boot, init
    runs "spm os recover" before the desktop starts.
  * Files under /system/config are YOURS: they are created when missing and never
    overwritten by an update.
  * A release that needs a newer kernel than the one you booted is refused (the kernel
    lives on the boot medium; boot a newer ISO first). Check with: spm os status.
  * Restart after an update so running programs (desktop, Settings...) pick up the new files.

Updating an install from before r63 (r61, r62)
  Old installs have no install record (os.tsv) and an old spm, so "spm upgrade" cannot
  update them. Bridge:
    1. boot a SwirlOS r63+ ISO with your disk attached (the kernel is on the ISO)
    2. spm install swirl-upgrade -f       (the old spm can do this)
    3. swirl-upgrade                      (check first with: swirl-upgrade check)
    4. restart
  swirl-upgrade compares every system file with the release BY CONTENT (no record to
  trust), downloads only what differs, and installs it under the same journal as
  "spm os upgrade". It also creates os.tsv, so from then on plain "spm os" works.
  Your files in /home/user and everything in /system/config stay untouched. Afterwards
  "spm remove swirl-upgrade" is safe - it owns only its own two files.

Files already in place
  If a package ships a file that is already on the disk with exactly the same content
  (for example DOOM or Cave Story, which the installer puts there), spm keeps it: no
  download, no conflict, and the package simply takes ownership. Each downloaded file
  needs one spare file slot (inode) until the swap; spm checks this first and tells you
  if the disk is out of slots instead of failing half way.
